      "page_title": "Script Skeleton, main & Helpers",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Skeleton, Top To Bottom",
          "ink": "accent"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Shebang, then `set -Eeuo pipefail`",
            "Constants, then `SCRIPT_DIR`, then sourcing",
            "`usage()`, `die()`, small helpers",
            "`main()` defined last of all functions",
            "`main \"$@\"` as the final line of the file"
          ],
          "style": "number"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "#!/usr/bin/env bash",
            "set -Eeuo pipefail",
            "readonly VERSION=1.4.0",
            "main() { parse_args \"$@\"; run; }",
            "main \"$@\"   # nothing below this"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Why A main Function"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Sourcing the file ==defines== but does not run",
            "Locals stay local — no global leakage",
            "`\"$@\"` forwards every arg, quoted, intact",
            "A truncated download cannot half-execute"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Find Your Own Directory"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "SCRIPT_DIR=$(cd -- \"$(dirname -- \\",
            "  \"${BASH_SOURCE[0]}\")\" && pwd)",
            "source \"$SCRIPT_DIR/lib.sh\""
          ],
          "note": "absolute path, caller-proof"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`$0` is what the caller typed, not the truth",
            "`cd` + `pwd` turns it absolute, once",
            "`--` guards a directory named like a flag",
            "Never resolve paths against the caller's `pwd`"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The die Helper"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "die() { echo \"$*\" >&2; exit 2; }",
            "[[ -f $cfg ]] || die \"no $cfg\"",
            "usage() { echo \"$USAGE\" >&2; }"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Logs To stderr, Data To stdout"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "log() { printf '%s\\n' \"$*\" >&2; }",
            "die() { log \"error: $*\"; exit 2; }",
            "report | jq .name   # logs intact"
          ],
          "note": "data on stdout, noise on stderr"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Humans read `stderr`, pipes read `stdout`",
            "`script.sh | jq` keeps working with logging on",
            "One `die()` — one **consistent** exit path",
            "Prefix each line with the script name for cron mail"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Parse Flags With getopts"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Flags, Validation, Exit Codes & Secrets",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "while getopts \":ho:\" f; do",
            "  case $f in",
            "  h) usage; exit 0 ;;",
            "  o) out=$OPTARG ;;",
            "  *) usage >&2; exit 2 ;;",
            "  esac; done"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`-h` always supported — usage to **stdout**",
            "Bad flag: usage to `stderr`, exit `2`",
            "`shift $((OPTIND-1))` before positionals",
            "Long `--flags` need a `case` loop, not `getopts`"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Validate Before You Act"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Required args checked before ==anything mutates==",
            "`[[ -r $cfg ]]` — readable, not merely present",
            "`command -v jq >/dev/null || die` per tool",
            "`((BASH_VERSINFO[0]>=4))` if you use arrays"
          ],
          "style": "check"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Exit Codes That Mean Something",
          "ink": "accent"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Code",
            "Means",
            "Example"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`0`",
              "success",
              "the only truth"
            ],
            [
              "`1`",
              "expected failure",
              "no match, no change"
            ],
            [
              "`2`",
              "usage error",
              "bad flag, missing arg"
            ],
            [
              "`>128`",
              "killed by signal",
              "`130` = Ctrl-C"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Secrets Never In argv"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`ps` shows **every** argument to every user",
            "`--password=x` leaks box-wide via `/proc`",
            "Pass by env var, file, or stdin instead",
            "`read -rs` for an interactive prompt",
            "Shell history keeps it long after you forget"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Tracing Leaks Secrets Too"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Portability, Safety Core & Shipping Tests",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "set +x                 # hush",
            "curl -H \"Auth: $TOK\" \"$url\"",
            "set -x                 # resume"
          ],
          "note": "CI keeps every traced line forever"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Portability Traps"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Tool",
            "GNU",
            "BSD / macOS"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`sed -i`",
              "bare `-i`",
              "`-i ''` needed"
            ],
            [
              "`date -d`",
              "yes",
              "`-v` instead"
            ],
            [
              "`readlink -f`",
              "yes",
              "use `grealpath`"
            ],
            [
              "`bash`",
              "5.x",
              "**3.2** by default"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Five-Line Safety Core"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "set -Eeuo pipefail",
            "IFS=$'\\n\\t'",
            "shopt -s inherit_errexit",
            "tmp=$(mktemp -d)",
            "trap 'rm -rf -- \"${tmp:?}\"' EXIT"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "shellcheck clean at `--severity=warning`",
            "Failure path tested, not just the happy one",
            "`--dry-run` prints every mutating command",
            "Idempotent: second run changes nothing"
          ],
          "style": "checkbox"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Test Before Production"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`bats-core` checks behaviour; shellcheck cannot",
            "`shfmt -i 2 -ci -d` gates formatting in CI",
            "Dry-run against a __scratch__ environment first",
            "Run the failure path: missing file, no permission",
            "`rm -- \"$f\"` so a leading dash isn't a flag"
          ],
          "style": "check"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Ship it or don't",
          "text": "Five lines of safety core, one `main \"$@\"`, one `die()`, zero secrets in argv. Everything else on this page is detail."
        }
      ]
    }
